INT. CLINIC WAITING ROOM - NIGHT

Vinyl chairs bolted in rows. A television mounted high plays a muted game show.

ANGELIQUE MBEKI, 40s, scrubs under a parka, sits with a clipboard. Her husband PETRUS, 40s, jiggles one knee. Their daughter ZANELE, 16, earbuds in, stares at the floor. An old man, MR. ABERNATHY GRIGGS, 80s, dozes. A NURSE, 30s, stands behind the glass.

The intercom crackles.

NURSE (O.S.)
Mbeki family?

Angelique stands.

ANGELIQUE
Yes.

NURSE (O.S.)
Dr. Okafor will see you now.

PETRUS
Finally.

ANGELIQUE
Petrus.

PETRUS
Four hours.

ANGELIQUE
Petrus.

He stops. Zanele takes out one earbud.

ZANELE
Is it Gran?

Nobody answers. The buzzer sounds on the inner door.

Mr. Griggs wakes, looks at them, and closes his eyes again.

Petrus shoves the nearest chair. It scrapes six inches across the tile, and the whole room hears it.

ANGELIQUE
(low)
Not here.

PETRUS
Then where?
